      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "We studied the cost-effectiveness of percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) plus beta-radiation (Radiotherapy) vs. PCI alone for treating in-stent restenosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS AND MATERIALS",
          "content": "We analyzed medical resource data collected at US sites during the Intimal Hyperplasia Inhibition with beta In-stent Trial (INHIBIT) to compare the incremental costs between Radiotherapy and PCI alone for each repeat target lesion revascularization (TLR) avoided. Costs were assigned for cardiac-related medical services using the Medicare payer perspective."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Radiotherapy increased initial inpatient costs by US$3360 (P<.001). In the Radiotherapy and PCI alone groups, the mean per-patient rates for TLR were 0.13 vs. 0.30 (P=.001), and mean per-patient total costs were US$19,286 vs. US$18,349 (P<.001), respectively. The incremental cost of Radiotherapy relative to PCI alone was US$5512 per TLR avoided, which compares favorably to the observed mean per-patient cost of treating restenosis (US$16,852)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "beta-radiation is a cost-effective adjunct to PCI in treating patients with in-stent restenosis."
        }
      ]},
